.TH a2si 3 (date) liba2i-(unreleased)
.
.
.SH Name
a2shh,
a2sh,
a2si,
a2sl,
a2sll,
\-
convert a string to a signed integer
.
.
.SH Library
String-to-integer library
.RI ( liba2i ,\~ \-la2i )
.
.
.SH Synopsis
.nf
.B #include <a2i.h>
.fi
.P
.B int
.SY a2shh(
.BI signed\~char\~*restrict\~ n ,
.BI QChar\~* s ,
.br
.BI QChar\~**_Nullable\~restrict\~ endp ,
.BI int\~ base ,
.br
.BI signed\~char\~ min \~=\~_Minof(signed\~char),
.BI signed\~char\~ max \~=\~_Maxof(signed\~char));
.YS .
.B int
.SY a2sh(
.BI short\~*restrict\~ n ,
.BI QChar\~* s ,
.br
.BI QChar\~**_Nullable\~restrict\~ endp ,
.BI int\~ base ,
.br
.BI short\~ min \~=\~_Minof(short),
.BI short\~ max \~=\~_Maxof(short));
.YS .
.B int
.SY a2si(
.BI int\~*restrict\~ n ,
.BI QChar\~* s ,
.br
.BI QChar\~**_Nullable\~restrict\~ endp ,
.BI int\~ base ,
.br
.BI int\~ min \~=\~_Minof(int),
.BI int\~ max \~=\~_Maxof(int));
.YS .
.B int
.SY a2sl(
.BI long\~*restrict\~ n ,
.BI QChar\~* s ,
.br
.BI QChar\~**_Nullable\~restrict\~ endp ,
.BI int\~ base ,
.br
.BI long\~ min \~=\~_Minof(long),
.BI long\~ max \~=\~_Maxof(long));
.YS .
.B int
.SY a2sll(
.BI long\~long\~*restrict\~ n ,
.BI QChar\~* s ,
.br
.BI QChar\~**_Nullable\~restrict\~ endp ,
.BI int\~ base ,
.br
.BI long\~long\~ min \~=\~_Minof(long\~long),
.BI long\~long\~ max \~=\~_Maxof(long\~long));
.YS .
.
.
.SH Description
These functions
convert the initial portion of the string pointed to by
.I s
to a signed integer of base
.IR base ,
ensure that the number is in the range
.RI [ min ,\~ max ]
and store it in
.IR *n .
.P
They are similar to
.BR strtoi (3bsd),
which itself is similar to
.BR strtol (3).
.P
The
.I base
must a value in the range
.RB [ 2 ,\~ 36 ],
or the special value
.BR 0 ,
which means either
.BR 8 ,
.BR 10 ,
or
.BR 16 ,
depending on the prefix
.RI ( \[dq]0\[dq] ,
none, or
.IR \[dq]0x\[dq] ,
respectively).
.P
If
.I endp
is not a null pointer,
these functions
store the address of the first invalid character in
.IR *endp .
.P
These functions always write to
.IR *n .
.IP \[bu] 3
The value parsed if it's in the range,
or the closest value in the range otherwise.
.IP \[bu] 3
.B 0
if no value was parsed,
or the closest value in the range if
.B 0
is not in the range.
.
.SS QChar
These functions are implemented with const-generic wrapper macros.
These decide the const-ness of
.I endp
depending on the const-ness of the input
.IR s .
So, if
.I s
is of type
.IR const\~char\~* ,
.I endp
will be of type
.IR const\~char\~** ;
and if
.I s
is of type
.IR char\~* ,
.I endp
will be of type
.IR char\~** .
.
.SS Default parameters
.I min
and/or
.I max
can be empty parameters,
in which case,
their default values are used.
.
.SS Leading white space
These functions reject leading white space.
.
.
.SH Return value
On success,
0 is returned.
On error,
-1 is returned,
and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.
.
.SH Errors
.TP
.B EINVAL
The
.I base
is not in the range
.RB [ 2 ,\~ 36 ]
nor
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B ECANCELED
The string did not contain any characters that were converted.
.TP
.B ERANGE
The parsed number was out of range.
.TP
.B ENOTSUP
The string contained non-numeric characters after a valid number.
.
.
.SH Examples
.EX
if (a2i(pid_t, &pid, s, NULL, 10, 1,) == \-1)
	goto err;
.EE
.
.
